{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfhsbNmV5y7AvAPNuEVvw","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I wrote the candidate and it passes the self-check with `OK`. The smoke fit's ELPD-LOO was −994.9, against the incumbent's −1003.0. That run was 100 draws on 1 chain, so it can't confirm convergence. It also flagged PSIS-LOO as unreliable: 9.6% of trials had Pareto k > 0.5, above the 1% tolerance. With only 100 draws that is probably noise, but neither the ELPD nor that flag will be settled until admission's full fit.\n\n**Model:** `length_normalised_chance_vs_motif`. It refines the incumbent, `bayesian_chance_vs_repeating_motif_2`.\n\n**Mechanism kept:** a sequence looks random to the extent that people's model of a fair coin explains it better than the regular generators. Their fair coin is believed to switch sides more than half the time. The regular generators are a coin that tends to switch or repeat, a coin biased towards heads or tails, and a short motif repeated with occasional slips. Each person still has their own decision sensitivity.\n\n**The one change:** people weigh that evidence per flip rather than in total. The log-evidence difference between the two sequences is divided by (n/8)^γ, where n is the pair's shared length. γ is fitted, with a Normal(0.5, 0.5) prior: 0 means total evidence, as in the incumbent, and 1 means fully per flip. In the code this is one new parameter and three lines inserted just before `score_diff`.\n\n**Why this change:** it targets the critique that people are more decisive on short pairs than on long ones, which survives the FDR correction (z = 2.45, q = 0.029). It may also help with long perfect alternations, which the incumbent condemns more than people do (q = 0.024). The novelty check against the live models was not run here; admission runs it.\n\nFiles are in `/scratch/users/kushinm/auto-psych/outer_loop_live/run3/data/subjective_randomness/experiment1/model_